    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "How many solutions does each equation have? State them and explain.\n(a) $x^{2}=225$\n(b) $x^{2}=0$\n(c) $x^{2}=-9$",
        "solution_text": "(a) $15^{2}=225$ and $(-15)^{2}=225$.\nThere are two solutions, $x=\\pm15$.\n(b) Only $0$ squares to give $0$. There is one solution, $x=0$.\n(c) A square is positive or zero, so it cannot equal $-9$. There is no solution."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Explain the difference between $\\sqrt{13}$ and the solutions of $x^{2}=13$. Leave the answers in exact form.",
        "solution_text": "The root sign gives the root that is positive or zero. Here it is $\\sqrt{13}$.\nThe equation has two solutions, $x=\\sqrt{13}$ and $x=-\\sqrt{13}$, written $x=\\pm\\sqrt{13}$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A square has area $324$ cm$^{2}$. Write an equation for its side length $s$ cm, state both solutions, and explain which answers the question.",
        "solution_text": "$s^{2}=324$\n$18^{2}=324$ and $(-18)^{2}=324$, so $s=\\pm18$.\nA side length cannot be negative. Reject $s=-18$; the side length is $18$ cm."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Find both solutions of $x^{2}=0.36$.",
        "solution_text": "$\\begin{align*}x^{2}&=0.36\\\\x&=\\pm\\sqrt{0.36}\\\\x&=\\pm0.6\\end{align*}$\nBoth signs work: $0.6\\times0.6=0.36$ and $(-0.6)\\times(-0.6)=0.36$."
      }
    ],
